-- title:
--   Zero scheme of r as a section of the trivial bundle
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme, let $\mathbb{1}$ denote the unit object of the monoidal category `X.Modules` of modules on $X$, let $t \colon \mathbb{1} \to \mathbb{1}$ be an endomorphism of this unit object, and let $r \in \Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X)$ be a global section. Assume that $t$, evaluated on global sections, sends the section `Scheme.Modules.toUnitSection ⊤ 1` — the unit $1 \in \Gamma(X,\mathcal{O}_X)$ read as a global section of $\mathbb{1}$ — to $r$ times that same section. The conclusion asserts an equality of quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$: the zero-scheme ideal `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal t`, defined as the infimum of all ideal sheaf data $J$ on $X$ whose ideal on each affine open $U$ contains the coefficient ideal of $t$ on $U$ (the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the range of the coefficient map of $t$ over $U$), coincides with `Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {r})`, the ideal sheaf data attached to the principal ideal $(r) \subseteq \Gamma(X,\mathcal{O}_X)$, whose ideal over an affine open $U$ is generated by the restriction $r|_U$.
--
--   This identifies the zero scheme of a section of an invertible module in an explicit trivialisation: the vanishing locus of $r$, viewed through an endomorphism of the trivial bundle multiplying $1$ by $r$, is the closed subscheme $V(r)$. It is used to compute products and powers of component ideal sheaves in terms of global functions, and is cited in the construction of invertible ideal sheaves of the form $(r)$ and in Euler-characteristic computations on modular curves.
